From: programista-zacny <5969291+programista-zacny@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: gadget: f_fs: drop the kiocb reference in
 ffs_aio_cancel(), fix async error paths

The AIO backport (3adb350) took ffs_aio_cancel() from v3.16, where the aio
core no longer refcounts kiocbs. In this 3.10 tree kiocb_cancel() still
does atomic_inc(&kiocb->ki_users) before calling ->ki_cancel() and relies
on the callback to drop that reference with aio_put_req(), as gadgetfs
does in ep_aio_cancel() (inode.c). ffs_aio_cancel() did not, so every
cancelled request leaked its kiocb together with its reference on the
endpoint file.

adbd always has reads pending on the bulk-out endpoint. When it exits
("adb root", "adb unroot", "adb tcpip", a crash, "stop adbd"), exit_aio()
cancels them, the kiocbs are never freed, ep1 is never released,
ffs->opened never reaches zero and functionfs_closed_callback() is never
called. The gadget stays enumerated with a dead function, the restarted
adbd cannot write its descriptors to an ep0 that is still FFS_ACTIVE, and
adb is gone until reboot. Seen on gts210ltexx: after "adb root" the host
kept 04e8:6860 with no USB disconnect and no adb device for six minutes.

While here, fix two more defects in the async submit path:
- the two failure branches after ffs_mutex_lock() returned without
  releasing epfile->mutex, blocking all later I/O on that endpoint;
- usb_ep_alloc_request() was called with GFP_KERNEL under eps_lock with
  interrupts disabled.
---
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/f_fs.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/f_fs.c
@@ -975,10 +975,11 @@
 		struct usb_request *req;
 
 		if (io_data->aio) {
-			req = usb_ep_alloc_request(ep->ep, GFP_KERNEL);
+			req = usb_ep_alloc_request(ep->ep, GFP_ATOMIC);
 			if (unlikely(!req)) {
 				ret = -ENOMEM;
 				spin_unlock_irq(&epfile->ffs->eps_lock);
+				mutex_unlock(&epfile->mutex);
 				goto error;
 			}
 
@@ -996,6 +997,7 @@
 			if (unlikely(ret)) {
 				usb_ep_free_request(ep->ep, req);
 				spin_unlock_irq(&epfile->ffs->eps_lock);
+				mutex_unlock(&epfile->mutex);
 				goto error;
 			}
 			ret = -EIOCBQUEUED;
@@ -1131,6 +1133,15 @@
 
 	spin_unlock_irq(&epfile->ffs->eps_lock);
 
+	/*
+	 * In this kernel kiocb_cancel() takes a reference on the kiocb before
+	 * calling ->ki_cancel() and expects the callback to drop it (see
+	 * ep_aio_cancel() in inode.c). Without this the kiocb is never freed,
+	 * its struct file is never released and the function can never be
+	 * closed and re-opened.
+	 */
+	aio_put_req(kiocb);
+
 	return value;
 }
 
